1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,320
Imagine spending hours
on an educational video.

2
00:00:02,550 --> 00:00:08,080
Researching the subject. Checking
the facts. Writing the script.

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:10,460
Creating original visuals.

4
00:00:10,874 --> 00:00:14,994
Then imagine making a short
video, uploading it to TikTok,

5
00:00:15,074 --> 00:00:17,974
and watching it get hundreds
of views within a few hours.

6
00:00:18,671 --> 00:00:22,911
Meanwhile, the carefully researched
long video struggles to find anyone.

7
00:00:23,461 --> 00:00:26,561
Of course, a single
example proves very little.

8
00:00:27,281 --> 00:00:29,461
The short might simply be more appealing.

9
00:00:30,201 --> 00:00:33,601
The algorithm might have found an
audience for one and not the other.

10
00:00:34,163 --> 00:00:36,443
But the contrast raises a question.

11
00:00:37,123 --> 00:00:39,423
What exactly is the internet rewarding?

12
00:00:41,653 --> 00:00:44,353
If the answer were simply
quality, or effort,

13
00:00:44,433 --> 00:00:46,893
the online world might
look very different.

14
00:00:47,595 --> 00:00:50,755
So perhaps the problem isn't
the people making the content.

15
00:00:51,195 --> 00:00:55,295
Perhaps it's the system deciding
what millions of people get to see.

16
00:00:55,606 --> 00:00:58,826
And that system has a problem
it can never fully solve.

17
00:00:59,466 --> 00:01:01,546
It can't see what a video is worth.

18
00:01:02,253 --> 00:01:04,633
It can't watch a careful
explanation and a pointless

19
00:01:04,863 --> 00:01:08,693
stunt, and decide which one
is more valuable to humanity.

20
00:01:09,517 --> 00:01:12,777
What it can see is what
people do. Whether they click.

21
00:01:12,857 --> 00:01:14,357
How long they watch.

22
00:01:14,757 --> 00:01:16,797
Whether they skip, like, or share.

23
00:01:17,405 --> 00:01:20,825
Every one of those numbers is
a clue about what people value.

24
00:01:21,205 --> 00:01:23,465
None of them is the value itself.

25
00:01:23,545 --> 00:01:26,885
And trouble starts when
the clue becomes the goal.

26
00:01:27,710 --> 00:01:31,190
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

27
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:33,430
It really helps the channel.

28
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:37,840
YouTube found that out early.

29
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:41,680
For years, its recommendations
leaned heavily on clicks.

30
00:01:42,147 --> 00:01:45,787
But a click only proves that
a title or a thumbnail worked.

31
00:01:46,387 --> 00:01:47,947
Not that the video did.

32
00:01:48,281 --> 00:01:52,741
So in 2012, YouTube shifted its
weight towards watch time instead.

33
00:01:53,476 --> 00:01:57,976
Four years later, its own engineers
explained why in a research paper.

34
00:01:58,656 --> 00:02:00,996
Ranking by clicks, they
wrote, often promotes

35
00:02:01,226 --> 00:02:03,756
deceptive videos that people don't finish.

36
00:02:04,616 --> 00:02:06,216
In a word, clickbait.

37
00:02:06,824 --> 00:02:10,184
Watch time is a much better
clue. It's harder to fake.

38
00:02:10,414 --> 00:02:11,984
But it still isn't quality.

39
00:02:13,453 --> 00:02:15,533
Think about how a video earns it.

40
00:02:16,193 --> 00:02:18,253
First, it has to stop you leaving.

41
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:22,513
A video about nuclear fusion
needs a few minutes to get good.

42
00:02:23,073 --> 00:02:26,733
It has to set up the problem, the
physics, and why anyone should care.

43
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:31,780
A video of someone dropping a watermelon
off a roof needs no introduction.

44
00:02:32,637 --> 00:02:34,057
No background knowledge.

45
00:02:34,137 --> 00:02:37,077
Nothing to remember. Not long to wait.

46
00:02:37,749 --> 00:02:40,609
The watermelon doesn't win
because it matters more.

47
00:02:41,249 --> 00:02:43,449
It wins because it's easier to consume.

48
00:02:44,247 --> 00:02:48,887
And a feed makes that advantage
enormous. Picture yourself scrolling.

49
00:02:49,657 --> 00:02:53,497
A scientist begins with a definition
and a graph about the climate.

50
00:02:54,177 --> 00:02:55,517
You could learn something.

51
00:02:55,597 --> 00:02:58,537
But you'd have to put
in some effort first.

52
00:02:59,229 --> 00:03:03,469
Swipe. A dog falls asleep
sitting up. Instant reward.

53
00:03:04,255 --> 00:03:10,455
Swipe. An absurd stunt. Swipe. An
argument. Swipe. Something unbelievable.

54
00:03:11,269 --> 00:03:15,489
When the next video is one swipe away,
trying something new costs nothing.

55
00:03:15,749 --> 00:03:17,469
And so does leaving.

56
00:03:18,129 --> 00:03:21,449
Short videos can be brilliant,
and long ones can be terrible.

57
00:03:22,209 --> 00:03:23,669
Length isn't the point.

58
00:03:24,371 --> 00:03:28,571
The point is that a creator now has a
few seconds to convince you to stay.

59
00:03:29,371 --> 00:03:32,571
A complicated idea may need
time to become interesting.

60
00:03:33,408 --> 00:03:37,088
A shocking image is interesting
before you even know what it is.

61
00:03:37,668 --> 00:03:39,928
Guess which one has the easier job.

62
00:03:42,368 --> 00:03:45,068
And why would a platform
care how long you stay?

63
00:03:45,928 --> 00:03:47,988
Because most of them
are paid by advertisers,

64
00:03:48,288 --> 00:03:50,348
and advertisers pay for attention.

65
00:03:51,149 --> 00:03:53,729
So picture two ways to
spend twenty minutes.

66
00:03:54,409 --> 00:03:57,449
A careful explanation of
a scientific discovery.

67
00:03:58,129 --> 00:04:00,569
Afterwards, something finally makes sense.

68
00:04:00,997 --> 00:04:05,677
Or a few hundred unrelated clips.
Some funny. Some irritating.

69
00:04:06,257 --> 00:04:08,337
Most forgotten by the next one.

70
00:04:08,841 --> 00:04:11,221
To the platform, both
look like engagement.

71
00:04:12,061 --> 00:04:14,061
Only one of them taught you something.

72
00:04:16,551 --> 00:04:17,971
Economists have a name for this.

73
00:04:18,201 --> 00:04:20,331
Goodhart's law.

74
00:04:20,851 --> 00:04:23,871
When a measure becomes a target,
it stops being a good measure.

75
00:04:24,787 --> 00:04:26,867
Clicks were a fair sign of interest...

76
00:04:27,087 --> 00:04:29,347
until titles were written to win them.

77
00:04:29,697 --> 00:04:32,217
Watch time is a fair sign of engagement...

78
00:04:32,497 --> 00:04:35,197
but it can't tell whether
the time was well spent.

79
00:04:36,115 --> 00:04:39,355
And then there's the
third clue. Reactions.

80
00:04:39,555 --> 00:04:41,915
A comment can mean a
post matters to people.

81
00:04:42,145 --> 00:04:43,835
Or that it made them furious.

82
00:04:44,663 --> 00:04:47,683
In 2018, Facebook changed
its feed to favour

83
00:04:47,813 --> 00:04:50,023
posts that got comments and reactions.

84
00:04:50,903 --> 00:04:54,623
Its own researchers later warned that
the change was rewarding outrage.

85
00:04:54,853 --> 00:05:00,463
And publishers complained that their most
divisive posts were the ones that spread.

86
00:05:01,156 --> 00:05:06,176
One political party in Poland told
Facebook that, by its own estimate, its

87
00:05:06,406 --> 00:05:10,516
posts had gone from about half negative
to about eighty per cent negative.

88
00:05:10,957 --> 00:05:14,537
And it said why. Because
of the change to the feed.

89
00:05:15,479 --> 00:05:17,639
That's Goodhart's law at full strength.

90
00:05:18,219 --> 00:05:21,019
The metric didn't just
measure what people posted.

91
00:05:21,559 --> 00:05:23,319
It changed what they posted.

92
00:05:26,169 --> 00:05:29,649
So is it the audience? Are
people simply getting stupider?

93
00:05:29,729 --> 00:05:30,869
That would be too easy.

94
00:05:31,099 --> 00:05:32,589
And probably wrong.

95
00:05:33,179 --> 00:05:35,279
Humans have always loved spectacle.

96
00:05:35,979 --> 00:05:37,699
Romans had gladiators.

97
00:05:38,439 --> 00:05:40,479
Crowds gathered for public executions.

98
00:05:41,399 --> 00:05:44,279
Newspapers learned long
ago that scandal sells.

99
00:05:45,036 --> 00:05:47,676
Even the famous claim that
our attention span is now

100
00:05:47,906 --> 00:05:51,136
shorter than a goldfish's
seems to come from nowhere.

101
00:05:51,831 --> 00:05:54,671
It appeared in a 2015
report from Microsoft,

102
00:05:55,051 --> 00:05:59,291
and nobody has found the study
behind it. Or the goldfish.

103
00:06:01,641 --> 00:06:04,621
What has changed is the
scale and the convenience.

104
00:06:05,441 --> 00:06:08,061
A broadcaster used to
choose what was on tonight.

105
00:06:08,864 --> 00:06:11,084
Now a system picks the next thing for you,

106
00:06:11,164 --> 00:06:14,004
every few seconds, based
on what you just did.

107
00:06:14,571 --> 00:06:16,791
You no longer search for entertainment.

108
00:06:17,791 --> 00:06:19,451
Entertainment searches for you.

109
00:06:20,002 --> 00:06:22,902
And if the easy option wins
just a little more often,

110
00:06:22,982 --> 00:06:26,422
across millions of people,
repeated thousands of times...

111
00:06:26,882 --> 00:06:28,782
that becomes a cultural force.

112
00:06:29,485 --> 00:06:31,885
The question isn't
whether people are stupid.

113
00:06:32,525 --> 00:06:35,745
It's whether the environment
lets them use their intelligence.

114
00:06:38,545 --> 00:06:41,485
A low view count doesn't
prove a video is good.

115
00:06:42,105 --> 00:06:44,225
A high one doesn't prove it's bad.

116
00:06:44,572 --> 00:06:47,672
But if popularity gets confused
with value, the easiest

117
00:06:47,902 --> 00:06:51,072
things to consume become
the easiest things to find.

118
00:06:51,392 --> 00:06:54,572
And everything else has to
fight just to be noticed.

119
00:06:57,222 --> 00:07:00,202
So what's the answer? Ban short videos?

120
00:07:00,282 --> 00:07:02,422
Blame people for watching silly clips?

121
00:07:03,144 --> 00:07:04,344
No.

122
00:07:04,462 --> 00:07:06,562
Sometimes you want astrophysics.

123
00:07:07,242 --> 00:07:12,422
Sometimes you want a cat behaving
like a broken robot. Both are fine.

124
00:07:13,095 --> 00:07:16,195
The problem is when one system
becomes the main doorway to what we

125
00:07:16,425 --> 00:07:20,495
see and learn, and the number it
chases isn't quite what we value.

126
00:07:21,042 --> 00:07:22,762
Platforms do know this.

127
00:07:23,342 --> 00:07:26,502
YouTube asks viewers in surveys
to rate videos from one to

128
00:07:26,732 --> 00:07:31,062
five stars, and tries to reward
time people say was well spent.

129
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:35,700
That's a better clue. But it's
still a guess about what you value,

130
00:07:35,780 --> 00:07:38,400
made by a system that's
paid by your attention.

131
00:07:39,822 --> 00:07:42,182
At the very least, we
can stop pretending that

132
00:07:42,412 --> 00:07:45,322
the most popular content
must be the best content.

133
00:07:45,719 --> 00:07:47,899
Because those are two different questions.

134
00:07:48,799 --> 00:07:51,059
What do people want to watch right now?

135
00:07:51,479 --> 00:07:54,439
And what do we want to still
be there ten years from now?

136
00:07:54,971 --> 00:08:00,131
We need jokes and discoveries, ten-second
distractions and ideas that take an hour.

137
00:08:00,771 --> 00:08:02,811
The goal isn't to get rid of the fun.

138
00:08:02,991 --> 00:08:07,111
It's to keep the slow and the
difficult from disappearing beneath it.

139
00:08:10,071 --> 00:08:12,371
There's a stranger way
to look at all this.

140
00:08:12,891 --> 00:08:16,331
The universe is thirteen
point eight billion years old,

141
00:08:16,411 --> 00:08:19,491
and our galaxy alone has
hundreds of billions of stars.

142
00:08:20,003 --> 00:08:22,243
Yet we've found no sign of anyone else.

143
00:08:22,643 --> 00:08:25,663
That's the Fermi paradox.
Where is everybody?

144
00:08:26,460 --> 00:08:28,940
Maybe civilisations destroy themselves.

145
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:31,840
Maybe the distances are too great.

146
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:34,060
Maybe life is just rare.

147
00:08:34,687 --> 00:08:36,347
Or maybe they invent smartphones.

148
00:08:36,577 --> 00:08:39,067
And recommendation algorithms.

149
00:08:39,297 --> 00:08:44,927
And spend the rest of their existence
watching increasingly ridiculous videos.

150
00:08:45,782 --> 00:08:47,742
Their spaceships stay unfinished.

151
00:08:48,422 --> 00:08:50,222
Their telescopes gather dust.

152
00:08:50,779 --> 00:08:54,699
And somewhere, around a distant
star, an entire civilisation is

153
00:08:54,929 --> 00:08:58,619
watching someone drop a thousand
rubber ducks into a swimming pool.

154
00:09:01,079 --> 00:09:04,719
There's no evidence that this
explains the silence of the universe.

155
00:09:05,479 --> 00:09:09,539
But the unsettling part is that the
algorithm wouldn't have to be evil.

156
00:09:10,158 --> 00:09:11,998
It wouldn't need to hate science.

157
00:09:12,758 --> 00:09:16,178
It would only need to be very good
at doing what it was built to do.

158
00:09:18,948 --> 00:09:22,048
We built the greatest
knowledge-sharing machine in history.

159
00:09:23,335 --> 00:09:27,075
Now we have to decide whether to
use it to understand the universe...

160
00:09:27,504 --> 00:09:30,534
or simply to pass the time
until the next video begins.
